        <register>
          <name>AHBAXI_CR</name>
          <description>Configures how many outstanding transfers the AXI-AHB bridges in front off the USB and Crypto blocks should allow. (See Synopsys AXI-AHB bridge documentation)</description>
          <addressOffset>0x08C</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0000FFFF</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ATHENA_RBCNT</name>
              <description>Number of outstanding read transactions to Athena block</description>
              <bitRange>[15:12]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ATHENA_WBCNT</name>
              <description>Number of outstanding write transactions to Athena block</description>
              <bitRange>[11:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>USB_RBCNT</name>
              <description>Number of outstanding read transactions to USB block</description>
              <bitRange>[7:4]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>USB_WBCNT</name>
              <description>Number of outstanding write transactions to USB block</description>
              <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AHBAPB_CR</name>
          <description>Configures the two AHB-APB bridges on S5 and S6</description>
          <addressOffset>0x090</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>APB1_POSTED</name>
              <description>Enables posted mode on the AHB-APB bridge, when set the AHB write cycle will complete before the APB write cycle completes.</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>APB0_POSTED</name>
              <description>Enables posted mode on the AHB-APB bridge, when set the AHB write cycle will complete before the APB write cycle completes.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>DFIAPB_CR</name>
          <description>MSS Corner APB interface controls</description>
          <addressOffset>0x098</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000002</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RESET</name>
              <description>Asserts the APB reset to the MSS corner, is asserted at MSS reset.</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CLOCKON</name>
              <description>Turns on the APB clock to the MSS Corner, is off at reset. Once corner blocks is configured the firmware may turn off the clock, but periodically should turn back on to allow refresh of TMR registers inside the corner block.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>GPIO_CR</name>
          <description>GPIO Blocks reset control</description>
          <addressOffset>0x09C</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x000F0703</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>GPIO2_DEFAULT</name>
              <description>Sets the reset value off the GPIO0 per byte Bit 0 controls GPIO0 [7:0] ,bit 1 GPIO[15:8] and bit 1 GPIO[23:16], and bit 3 GPIO[31:24]</description>
              <bitRange>[23:20]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>GPIO2_SOFT_RESET_SELECT</name>
              <description>This signal selects whether the associated byte is reset by soft reset or the the MSS_GPIO_RESET_N signal from the FPGA fabric. The allowed values are: • 0: Selects MSS_GPIO_RESET_N signal from the FPGA fabric. • 1: Selects the GPIO to be reset by the GPIO block soft reset signal . Bit 0 controls GPIO0 [7:0] ,bit 1 GPIO[15:8] and bit 1 GPIO[23:16], and bit 3 GPIO[31:24] The master MSS reset will also reset the GPIO register if not configured to use fabric reset.</description>
              <bitRange>[19:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>GPIO1_DEFAULT</name>
              <description>Sets the reset value off the GPIO0 per byte Bit 0 controls GPIO0 [7:0] ,bit 1 GPIO[15:8] and bit 2 GPIO[23:16]</description>
              <bitRange>[14:12]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>GPIO1_SOFT_RESET_SELECT</name>
              <description>This signal selects whether the associated byte is reset by soft reset or the the MSS_GPIO_RESET_N signal from the FPGA fabric. The allowed values are: • 0: Selects MSS_GPIO_RESET_N signal from the FPGA fabric. • 1: Selects the GPIO to be reset by the GPIO block soft reset signal . Bit 0 controls GPIO0 [7:0] ,bit 1 GPIO[15:8] and bit 2 GPIO[23:16] The master MSS reset will also reset the GPIO register if not configured to use fabric reset.</description>
              <bitRange>[10:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>GPIO0_DEFAULT</name>
              <description>Sets the reset value off the GPIO0 per byte Bit 0 controls GPIO0 [7:0] , and bit 1 GPIO[15:8]</description>
              <bitRange>[5:4]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>GPIO0_SOFT_RESET_SELECT</name>
              <description>This signal selects whether the associated byte is reset by soft reset or the the MSS_GPIO_RESET_N signal from the FPGA fabric. The allowed values are: • 0: Selects MSS_GPIO_RESET_N signal from the FPGA fabric. • 1: Selects the GPIO to be reset by the GPIO block soft reset signal . Bit 0 controls GPIO0 [7:0] , and bit 1 GPIO[15:8] The master MSS reset will also reset the GPIO register if not configured to use fabric reset.</description>
              <bitRange>[1:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>MAC0_CR</name>
          <description>MAC0 configuration register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0A4</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SPEED_MODE</name>
              <description>Current speed mode on the MAC</description>
              <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>MAC1_CR</name>
          <description>MAC1 configuration register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0A8</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SPEED_MODE</name>
              <description>Current speed mode on the MAC</description>
              <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>USB_CR</name>
          <description>USB Configuration register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0AC</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000004</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LPI_CARKIT_EN</name>
              <description>Set when entry is made into CarKit mode and cleared on exit from CarKit mode.</description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>POWERDOWN</name>
              <description>Indicates that the USB CLK may be stopped to save power. Derived from combination of signals from CLK &amp; XCLK flip-flops, AVALID, VBUSVALID and LINESTATE. When asserted the USB clock into the core is stopped.</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>POWERDOWN_ENABLE</name>
              <description>When '1' will stops the clock to the USB core when the core asserts its POWERDOWN output. For G4 compatability this bit defaults to 0.</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DDR_SELECT</name>
              <description>Configures USB for Single-Data Rate(SDR) mode or Double-Data Rate(DDR) mode. 0 - SDR Mode is selected 1 - DDR Mode is selected (Not supported in G5 or G5)</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>MESH_CR</name>
          <description>Crypto Mesh control and status register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0B0</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0000004E</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>OKAY</name>
              <description>Indicates that the Mesh is functioning correctly. Will be set approximately 520 clock cycles after mesh started and stay set as long as the mesh is not detecting any errors.</description>
              <bitRange>[25:25]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MESH_ERROR</name>
              <description>Indicates that Mesh detected an error. Cleared by writing a '1'</description>
              <bitRange>[24:24]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>INJECT_ERROR</name>
              <description>When set will inject an error in the mesh</description>
              <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HOLD</name>
              <description>Sets the amount of time that the mesh is held active for, actual hold time includes up to 256 us of random variation. Minimum Time = 1 + 256 * value us Maximum Time = 1 + 256 * (1+value) us Value must be greater than 0</description>
              <bitRange>[12:1]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>START</name>
              <description>Writing a 1 will start the Mesh System</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>MESH_SEED_CR</name>
          <description>Crypto mesh seed and update rate</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0B4</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0xC7005EED</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CLKRATE</name>
              <description>Sets the rate that the mesh value is changed. Rate = AHBCLK/(clkrate+1). Rate must be less than 1MHz, setting slower will reduce power consumption.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:24]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SEED</name>
              <description>Sets the mesh seed value, any value may be used, zero should be avoided</description>
              <bitRange>[22:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>ENVM_CR</name>
          <description>ENVM AHB Controller setup</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0B8</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x4005004F</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TIMER</name>
              <description>Sets the duration of the timer used to detect a non response of slow response from the PNVM on C and R bus accesses. Timer Duration = Value * (1000/AHBFREQMHZ) 0x00: Timer dissabled. If the timer expires the AHB cycle is terminates using the HRESP protocol</description>
              <bitRange>[31:24]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>INTERRUPT_ENABLE</name>
              <description>Enable the ENVM interrupt</description>
              <bitRange>[18:18]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLOWREAD</name>
              <description>When '1' the controller will initate separate ENVM reads for all reads. No buffering or speculative operations will be carried out. When performing word reads incrementing through PNVM each location will be read twice (intended for test use)</description>
              <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>READAHEAD</name>
              <description>Enables "readahead" on the ENVM controller. The controller will automatically read the next PNVM location as soon as possible ahead of the AHB request. This will improve read performance when incrementing though memory as the NVM reads and AHB cycles are pipelined. When set non incrementing accesses will take longer as the controller may be in the process of reading the next address and the PNVM cycle needs to complete prior to starting the required read</description>
              <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CLOCK_SUPPRESS</name>
              <description>When set suppresses clock edge between C-Bus access cycles so that they appear as consecutive access cycles.</description>
              <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CLOCK_CONTINUOUS</name>
              <description>When '1' the PNVM clock will be always generated, and not stopped between access cycles. Setting this will increase access latency but mean that the PNVM clock operates at a stable rate.</description>
              <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CLOCK_OKAY</name>
              <description>Indicates the eNVM is running at the configured divider rate.</description>
              <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CLOCK_PERIOD</name>
              <description>Sets the number of AHB cycles used to generate the PNVM clock, Clock period = (Value+1) * (1000/AHBFREQMHZ) Value must be 1 to 63 (0 defaults to 15) e.g. 11 will generate a 40ns period 25MHz clock if the AHB clock is 250MHz 15 will generate a 40ns period 25MHz clock if the AHB clock is 400MHz</description>
              <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>RESERVED_BC</name>
          <description>Reserved</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0BC</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields />
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>QOS_PERIPHERAL_CR</name>
          <description>QOS Athena, USB &amp; MMC Configuration</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0C0</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TRACE_WRITE</name>
              <description>Sets the QOS value from the specified device into the switch</description>
              <bitRange>[31:28]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TRACE_READ</name>
              <description>Sets the QOS value from the specified device into the switch</description>
              <bitRange>[27:24]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MMC_WRITE</name>
              <description>Sets the QOS value from the specified device into the switch</description>
              <bitRange>[23:20]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MMC_READ</name>
              <description>Sets the QOS value from the specified device into the switch</description>
              <bitRange>[19:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>USB_WRITE</name>
              <description>Sets the QOS value from the specified device into the switch</description>
              <bitRange>[15:12]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>USB_READ</name>
              <description>Sets the QOS value from the specified device into the switch</description>
              <bitRange>[11:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ATHENA_WRITE</name>
              <description>Sets the QOS value from the specified device into the switch</description>
              <bitRange>[7:4]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
