        </register>
        <register>
          <name>SRS15</name>
          <description>SRS15 - Host Control #2 / Auto CMD Error Status</description>
          <addressOffset>0x23C</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>PVE</name>
              <description>PVE - Preset Value Enable

 Setting this bit to 1 triggers an automatically update of SRS11.SDCFSL, SRS11.SDCFSH, SRS11.CGS, SRS15.DSS registers by the host. Values for an update are taken from SRS24 - SRS27 and depends on SRS15.UMS.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>A64B</name>
              <description>A64B - 64-bit Addressing

 Specifies the addressing mode for DMA ending. This field is ignored when SRS15.HV4E=0.
 0 - 32-bit addressing
 1 - 64-bit addressing</description>
              <bitRange>[29:29]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HV4E</name>
              <description>HV4E - Host Version 4.00 Enable

 Selects backward (SD Host 3.00 Version) compatibility mode or SD Host 4.00 Version mode.
 0 - Version 3.00
 1 - Version 4.00

 The software can select system address register SRS00 (when this bit is 0) or SRS23 / SRS22 (when this bit is 1) for the SDMA engine.</description>
              <bitRange>[28:28]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SCS</name>
              <description>SCS - Sampling Clock Select (UHS-I only)

 The host updates this bit when the tuning procedure is finished. If this bit is set to 1, this means that the tuning procedure is successfully completed.
 Otherwise it means that procedure failed and clock tuning logic is disabled.
 This bit is valid only after the procedure is finished.Writing 1 will be ignored.
 Writing 0 will reset and disable tuning block.</description>
              <bitRange>[23:23]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EXTNG</name>
              <description>EXTNG - Execute Tuning (UHS-I only)

 This register controls tuning procedure.
 The procedure starts when the bit is set 1.
 The procedure can be aborted when the bit is cleared.

 The bit is read 1 while the procedure is in progress, and 0 when the procedure is finished.
 SCS = 0, EXTNG = 0 - Reset and disable clock tuning logic
 SCS = 0, EXTNG = 1 - Reset and restart tuning process
 SCS = 1, EXTNG = 0 - Stop tuning procedure
 SCS = 1, EXTNG = 1 - Start retuning (without clock tuning logic reset)</description>
              <bitRange>[22:22]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DSS</name>
              <description>DSS - Driver Strength Select (UHS-I only)

 This bit controls the electric parameters of I/O driver. Up to 4 configurations of I/O driver settings can be implemented:
 00 - Driver Type B (default)
 01 - Driver Type A
 10 - Driver Type C
 11 - Driver Type D
 The bit is ignored when the V18SE is cleared.</description>
              <bitRange>[21:20]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>V18SE</name>
              <description>V18SE - 1.8V Signaling Enable (UHS-I only)

 This bit controls I/O signaling voltage level.
 If the bit is 0 or 1, the I/O uses the 3.3V or 1.8V signaling, respectively.
 The SW driver will set this bit 1 when UHS-I mode.
 Depend on the selected SD interface mode, the software will set this field as follows:
 - 0 - for Default Speed, High Speed mode
 - 1 - for UHS-I mode</description>
              <bitRange>[19:19]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>UMS</name>
              <description>UMS - UHS Mode Select

 Used to select one of UHS-I modes .
 000b - SDR12
 001b - SDR25
 010b - SDR50
 011b - SDR104
 100b - DDR50
 101b - Reserved
 110b - Reserved
 111b - Reserved

 The selected UHS-I mode (when value is in range 000b-100b) will be ignored when V18SE is 0.</description>
              <bitRange>[18:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <name>UHS_Mode</name>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>SDR12</name>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>SDR25</name>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>SDR50</name>
                  <value>2</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>SDR104</name>
                  <value>3</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>DDR50</name>
                  <value>4</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>Reserved1</name>
                  <value>5</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>Reserverd2</name>
                  <value>6</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>Reserved3</name>
                  <value>7</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CNIACE</name>
              <description>CNIACE - Command Not Issued By Auto CMD12 Error

 When read as 1, the command was not executed by the Host due to the previous Auto CMD12 error.
 When Host detects any error during Auto-CMD12, then all further command generation attempts are blocked. The software reset sequence is needed for recovery.
 Bit is set to 0, when Auto CMD23 Error is detected (any of bits SRS15[4:1] is set).</description>
              <bitRange>[7:7]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ACRE</name>
              <description>ACRE - Auto CMD Response Error

 When read as 1, means an error is detected in response to Auto Command.</description>
              <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ACIE</name>
              <description>ACIE - Auto CMD Index Error

 When read as 1, means that Command Index error occurred in the Auto CMD response.</description>
              <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ACEBE</name>
              <description>ACEBE - Auto CMD End Bit Error

 When read as 1, indicates that the end bit of the Auto-CMD response is 0.</description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ACCE</name>
              <description>ACCE - Auto CMD CRC Error

 When read as 1, indicates a CRC error was detected in the Auto CMD response or conflict on the CMD lines is detected:
 ACCE(SRS15.2) = 0, ACTE(SRS15.1) = 0 - No error
 ACCE(SRS15.2) = 0, ACTE(SRS15.1) = 1 - Auto CMD Timeout error detected
 ACCE(SRS15.2) = 1, ACTE(SRS15.1) = 0 - Auto CMD CRC error detected
 ACCE(SRS15.2) = 1, ACTE(SRS15.1) = 1 - Conflict on the CMD line detected</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ACTE</name>
              <description>ACTE - Auto CMD Timeout Error

 When read as 1, indicates that there was no response within 64 SDCLK clock cycles from the end bit of the Auto CMD or conflict on the CMD lines is detected (see table in SRS15.ACCE field description).
 If this bit is set to 1, the other error status bits (SRS15[4:2]) are meaningless.</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ACNE</name>
              <description>ACNE - Auto CMD12 Not Executed

 When set to 1, means the host cannot issue Auto CMD12 due to some error. If this bit is set to 1, other error status bits (SRS15[4:1]) are meaningless.
 Bit is updated with 0, when Auto CMD23 Error is detected (any of bits SRS15[4:1] is set).</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>SRS16</name>
          <description>SRS16 - Capabilities #1
 HWINIT Register - Note this register is hardware initialized after reset and the value read back will match the IP configuration.</description>
          <addressOffset>0x240</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x176AC8B2</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SLT</name>
              <description>SLT - Slot Type

 These bits inform what type of slot is provided.
 00 - Removable Card Slot
 01 - Embedded Slot for One Device
 10 - Shared Bus Slot
 11 - Reserved</description>
              <bitRange>[31:30]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>AIS</name>
              <description>AIS - Asynchronous Interrupt Support

 0 - not supported
 1 - supported</description>
              <bitRange>[29:29]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>A64S</name>
              <description>A64S - 64-bit System Addressing Support

 0 - not supported
 1 - supported</description>
              <bitRange>[28:28]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS16_HWINIT1</name>
              <description>HWINIT field</description>
              <bitRange>[27:27]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>VS18</name>
              <description>VS18 - Voltage Support 1.8V

 0 - not supported
 1 - supported</description>
              <bitRange>[26:26]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>VS30</name>
              <description>VS30 - Voltage Support 3.0V

 0 - not supported
 1 - supported</description>
              <bitRange>[25:25]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>VS33</name>
              <description>VS33 - Voltage Support 3.3V

 0 - not supported
 1 - supported</description>
              <bitRange>[24:24]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS</name>
              <description>SRS - Suspend / Resume Support

 0 - not supported
 1 - supported

 The host controller does not support Suspend / Resume mechanism.</description>
              <bitRange>[23:23]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DMAS</name>
              <description>DMAS - SDMA Support

 0 - not supported
 1 - supported
 This bit defines whether the SDMA is supported in the slot. There is a separate IMPLEMENT_DMA generic parameter which defines whether the DMA is physically implemented inside host. If DMAS is set to 1 for any card slot, then the IMPLEMENT_DMA would also be set to 1.</description>
              <bitRange>[22:22]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HSS</name>
              <description>HSS - High Speed Support

 0 - not supported
 1 - supported</description>
              <bitRange>[21:21]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADMA1S</name>
              <description>ADMA1S - ADMA1 Support

 0 - not supported
 1 - supported</description>
              <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADMA2S</name>
              <description>ADMA2S - ADMA2 Support

 0 - not supported
 1 - supported</description>
              <bitRange>[19:19]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EDS8</name>
              <description>8EDS - 8-bit Embedded Device Support

 0 - not supported
 1 - supported
 If this bit is 0, the SRS10.EDTW register is not implemented.</description>
              <bitRange>[18:18]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MBL</name>
              <description>SRS16.MBL - Max Block Length

 This value indicates the maximum block size that can be transferred by the host. Three sizes can be defined as indicated below:
 00b - 512 Bytes
 01b - 1024 Bytes
 10b - 2048 Bytes
 11b - Reserved
 The physical FIFO buffer size is defined by the separate FIFODEPTH generic parameter, and the physical buffer size is equal to 2^FIFODEPTH * 8 bytes. Therefore, the Maximum Block Size defined by MBL will always be less or equal to the physical buffer size.</description>
              <bitRange>[17:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BCSDCLK</name>
              <description>BCSDCLK - Base Clock Frequency for SD Clock

 Field defines the base clock frequency for the SD Clock in 1MHz units. The base clock is the clock sourced to sdmclk pin of the host. The maximum clock frequency supported is between 10MHz to 255MHz.
 If BCSDCLK = 0, the Host System has to obtain the clock information via another method (i.e. not defined by the specification).</description>
              <bitRange>[15:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TCU</name>
              <description>TCU - Timeout Clock Unit

 Field defines the frequency unit for the SRS16.TCF.
 0 - kHz
 1 - MHz</description>
              <bitRange>[7:7]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS16_HWINIT0</name>
              <description>HWINIT field</description>
              <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TCF</name>
              <description>TCF - Timeout Clock Frequency

 Defines the base clock frequency used to detect Data Timeout Error. The SRS16.TCU bit determines the unit used.
 111111b - 63kHz(SRS16.TCU=0) or 63MHz(SRS16.TCU=1)
 111110b - 63kHz(SRS16.TCU=0) or 63MHz(SRS16.TCU=1)
 ...
 000001b - 1kHz(SRS16.TCU=0) or 1MHz(SRS16.TCU=1)
 000000b - Host System has to obtain the clock information via another method (i.e. not defined by the spec).</description>
              <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>SRS17</name>
          <description>SRS17 - Capabilities #2HWINIT Register - Note this register is hardware initialized after reset and the value read back will match the IP configuration.</description>
          <addressOffset>0x244</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x10000077</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SRS17_HWINIT3</name>
              <description>HWINIT field</description>
              <bitRange>[31:29]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>VDD2S</name>
              <description>VDD2S - VDD2 Supported

 Information about support or lack of support of optional VDD2.</description>
              <bitRange>[28:28]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS17_HWINIT2</name>
              <description>HWINIT field</description>
              <bitRange>[27:24]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CLKMPR</name>
              <description>CLKMPR - Clock Multiplier

 This field is to be 0 (fixed), as the Clock Multiplier is not supported.</description>
              <bitRange>[23:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTNGM</name>
              <description>RTNGM - Re-Tuning Modes

 Depending on the retuning method, the some restrictions are assumed for the data length between re-tunings.
 The core can work with supporting one of the three method:
 0 - Mode1: The software driver will use timer to calculate when the re-tuning is to be rerun. The data length between operations is limited to the 4MB.
 1 - Mode2: The driver will use either the re-tuning request (external input pin uhsi_retune_req is used for this purpose) or timer to predict when next retuning should be performed. The data length between operations is limited to the 4MB.
 2 - Mode3: This mode is similar to the mode2 with one exception. The core is able to perform auto retuning during the transmission, so data length limitation is not exists. Mode 3 is currently not supported.The driver can configure the timer by getting the RTNGCNT.This field is to be 0 or 1, because the mode 3 is not supported.</description>
              <bitRange>[15:14]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>UTSM50</name>
              <description>UTSM50 - Use Tuning for SDR50

 1 - tuning operation is necessary in SDR50 mode
 0 - tuning operation is not necessary in SDR50 mode</description>
              <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS17_HWINIT1</name>
              <description>HWINIT field</description>
              <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTNGCNT</name>
              <description>RTNGCNT - Timer Count for Re-Tuning

 These bits contain initial value for timer used to starting periodically Re-Tuning Operation.
 0h - Re-Tuning Timer disabled
 1h - 1 second
 ...
 n - 2^(n-1) seconds
 ...
 Bh - 1024 seconds
 Eh-Ch - Reserved
 Fh - Obtain this info in other way</description>
              <bitRange>[11:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS17_HWINIT0</name>
              <description>HWINIT field</description>
              <bitRange>[7:7]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DRVD</name>
              <description>DRVD - 1.8V Line Driver Type D Supported

 1 - Driver Type D supported
 0 - Driver Type D not supported</description>
              <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DRVC</name>
              <description>DRVC - 1.8V Line Driver Type C Supported

 1 - Driver Type C supported
 0 - Driver Type C not supported</description>
              <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DRVA</name>
              <description>DRVA - 1.8V Line Driver Type A Supported

 1 - Driver Type A supported
 0 - Driver Type A not supported</description>
              <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>UHSII</name>
              <description>UHSII - UHS-II Supported

 1 - UHS-II supported
 0 - UHS-II not supported</description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DDR50</name>
              <description>DDR50 - DDR50 Supported

 1 - DDR50 mode supported
 0 - DDR50 mode not supported</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SDR104</name>
              <description>SDR104 - SDR104 Supported

 1 - SDR104 mode supported
 0 - SDR104 mode not supported</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SDR50</name>
              <description>SDR50 - SDR50 Supported

 1 - SDR50 mode supported
 0 - SDR50 mode not supported</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>SRS18</name>
          <description>SRS18 - Capabilities #3HWINIT Register - Note this register is hardware initialized after reset and the value read back will match the IP configuration.</description>
          <addressOffset>0x248</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00202020</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SRS18_HWINIT0</name>
              <description>HWINIT field</description>
              <bitRange>[31:24]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MC18</name>
              <description>MC18 - Maximum Current for 1.8V

 0 - Host System has to obtain the current value via another method
 1 - 4 mA
 2 - 8 mA
 3 - 12 mA
 ...
 255 - 1020 mA</description>
              <bitRange>[23:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MC30</name>
              <description>MC30 - Maximum Current for 3.0V

 0 - Host System has to obtain the current value via another method
 1 - 4 mA
 2 - 8 mA
 3 - 12 mA
 ...
 255 - 1020 mA</description>
              <bitRange>[15:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MC33</name>
              <description>MC33 - Maximum Current for 3.3V

 0 - Host System has to obtain the current value via another method
 1 - 4 mA
 2 - 8 mA
 3 - 12 mA
 ...
 255 - 1020 mA</description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>SRS19</name>
          <description>SRS19 - Capabilities #4
 HWINIT Register - Note this register is hardware initialized after reset and the value read back will match the IP configuration.</description>
          <addressOffset>0x24C</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000020</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SRS19_HWINIT0</name>
              <description>HWINIT field</description>
              <bitRange>[31:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MC18V2</name>
              <description>MC18V2 - Maximum Current for 1.8V VDD2

 0 - Host System has to obtain the current value via another method
 1 - 4 mA
 2 - 8 mA
 3 - 12 mA
 ...
 255 - 1020 mA</description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>SRS20</name>
          <description>SRS20 - Force Event

 Each field of this register is related to the specific error status. Writing 1 to field will set the status error. This function is provided for SW debug purpose.</description>
          <addressOffset>0x250</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ERESP_FE</name>
              <description>ERESP_FE - Force Response Error Event</description>
              <bitRange>[27:27]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ETUNE_FE</name>
              <description>ETUNE_FE - Force Tuning Error Event</description>
              <bitRange>[26:26]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EADMA_FE</name>
              <description>EADMA_FE - Force ADMA Error Event</description>
              <bitRange>[25:25]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EAC_FE</name>
              <description>EAC_FE - Force Auto CMD Error Event</description>
              <bitRange>[24:24]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ECL_FE</name>
              <description>ECL_FE - Force Current Limit Error Event</description>
              <bitRange>[23:23]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EDEB_FE</name>
              <description>EDEB_FE - Force Data End Bit Error Event</description>
              <bitRange>[22:22]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EDCRC_FE</name>
              <description>EDCRC_FE - Force Data CRC Error Event</description>
              <bitRange>[21:21]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EDT_FE</name>
              <description>EDT_FE - Force Data Timeout Error Event</description>
              <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ECI_FE</name>
              <description>ECI_FE - Force Command Index Error Event</description>
              <bitRange>[19:19]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ECEB_FE</name>
              <description>ECEB_FE - Force Command End Bit Error Event</description>
              <bitRange>[18:18]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ECCRC_FE</name>
              <description>ECCRC_FE - Force Command CRC Error Event</description>
              <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ECT_FE</name>
              <description>ECT_FE - Force Command Timeout Error Event</description>
              <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CNIACE_FE</name>
              <description>CNIACE_FE - Force Command Not Issued By Auto CMD12 Error Event</description>
              <bitRange>[7:7]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ACIE_FE</name>
              <description>ACIE_FE - Force Auto CMD Index Error Event</description>
              <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ACEBE_FE</name>
              <description>ACEBE_FE - Force Auto CMD End Bit Error Event</description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ACCE_FE</name>
              <description>ACCE_FE - Force Auto CMD CRC Error Event</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ACTE_FE</name>
              <description>ACTE_FE - Force Auto CMD Timeout Error Event</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ACNE_FE</name>
              <description>ACNE_FE - Force Auto CMD12 Not Executed Event</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>SRS21</name>
          <description>SRS21 - ADMA Error Status</description>
          <addressOffset>0x254</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>EADMAL</name>
              <description>EADMAL - ADMA Length Mismatch Error

 This bit is set when:
 - total data length specified in ADMA descriptors is different from that specified by the Block Count and Block Length fields (if Block Count Enable is set).
 - total data length cannot be divided into complete blocks of specified length (if Block Count Enable is not set).</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EADMAS</name>
              <description>EADMAS - ADMA Error State

 The value of this field reflects the state of the ADMA state machine. The possible values are:
 00b - ST_STOP (ADMA Stopped)
 01b - ST_FDS (Fetching descriptor)
 10b - not used
 11b - ST_TRF (Transfer data)</description>
              <bitRange>[1:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>SRS22</name>
          <description>SRS22 ADMA/SDMA System Address 1</description>
          <addressOffset>0x258</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DMASA1</name>
              <description>DMASA1 - ADMA System Address

 This field contains the physical address of the currently processed ADMA descriptor or SDMA system address. The Host Driver will set this register with the descriptors table base address before it starts the ADMA transfers. The Host Driver should not write this register while the data transfer is active.
 While the ADMA engine is processing the descriptors list, the ADMASA value is always incremented to point the next descriptor to be fetched.
 If the ADMA Error occurs, the register holds the descriptor address depending on the ADMA Error State (SRS21.EADMAS) register value, as listed in the table below:
 00b - Points next of the error descriptor
 01b - Points the error descriptor
 10b - not used
 11b - Points next of the error descriptor

 The host ADMA engine ignores 2 or 3 least significant bits in this register when the 32-bit or 64-bit addressing is active, respectively.
 If SRS15.HV4E is set 1 and SDMA engine is selected, this field is used instead of SRS00 to define system memory address. This register incremented and points to the next memory location that will be accessed.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>SRS23</name>
          <description>SRS23 ADMA/SDMA System Address 2</description>
          <addressOffset>0x25C</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DMASA2</name>
              <description>DMASA2 - ADMA System Address #2

 In ADMA mode, if 64-bit addressing is enabled (SRS15.A64B=1), this field holds bits 63-32 of the physical address pointing on ADMA descriptor table.
 In SDMA mode, if host compatibility with version 4.x and 64-bit addressing are enabled (SRS15.HV4E=1 and SRS15.A64B=1), this field holds bits 63-32 of system address.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>SRS24</name>
          <description>SRS24 - Preset Value (Default Speed)

 SRS24[31:16] - Default Speed if:
 SRS15.V18SE=0
 SRS10.HSE=0

 HWINIT Register - Note this register is hardware initialized after reset and the value read back will match the IP configuration.</description>
          <addressOffset>0x260</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x000400FA</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SRS24_DSSPV_31_30</name>
              <description>DSSPV## - Driver Strength Select - Preset Value

 This field can be used by the software to update SRS15.DSS.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:30]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS24_HWINIT1</name>
              <description>HWINIT field</description>
              <bitRange>[29:26]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS24_SDCFSPV_25_16</name>
              <description>SDCFSPV## - SDCLK Clock Frequency Select - Preset Value

 This field can be used by the software to update SRS11.SDCFSH and SRS11.SDCFSL.</description>
              <bitRange>[25:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS24_HWINIT0</name>
              <description>HWINIT field</description>
              <bitRange>[15:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>SRS25</name>
          <description>SRS25 - Preset Value (High Speed and SDR12)

 SRS25[15:0] - High Speed if:
 SRS15.V18SE=0
 SRS.HSE=1

 SRS25[31:16] - SDR12 if:
 SRS15.V18SE=1
 SRS15.UMS=000b

 HWINIT Register - Note this register is hardware initialized after reset and the value read back will match the IP configuration.</description>
          <addressOffset>0x264</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00040002</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SRS25_DSSPV_31_30</name>
              <description>DSSPV## - Driver Strength Select - Preset Value

 This field can be used by the software to update SRS15.DSS.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:30]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS25_HWINIT1</name>
              <description>HWINIT field</description>
              <bitRange>[29:26]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS25_SDCFSPV_25_16</name>
              <description>SDCFSPV## - SDCLK Clock Frequency Select - Preset Value

 This field can be used by the software to update SRS11.SDCFSH and SRS11.SDCFSL.</description>
              <bitRange>[25:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS25_DSSPV_15_14</name>
              <description>DSSPV## - Driver Strength Select - Preset Value

 This field can be used by the software to update SRS15.DSS.</description>
              <bitRange>[15:14]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS25_HWINIT0</name>
              <description>HWINIT field</description>
              <bitRange>[13:10]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS25_SDCFSPV_09_00</name>
              <description>SDCFSPV## - SDCLK Clock Frequency Select - Preset Value

 This field can be used by the software to update SRS11.SDCFSH and SRS11.SDCFSL.</description>
              <bitRange>[9:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>SRS26</name>
          <description>SRS26 - Preset Value (SDR25 and SDR50)

 SRS26[15:0] - SDR25 if:
 SRS15.V18SE=1
 SRS15.UMS=001b

 SRS26[31:16] - SDR50 if:
 SRS15.V18SE=1
 SRS15.UMS=010b

 HWINIT Register - Note this register is hardware initialized after reset and the value read back will match the IP configuration.</description>
          <addressOffset>0x268</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00010002</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SRS26_DSSPV_31_30</name>
              <description>DSSPV## - Driver Strength Select - Preset Value

 This field can be used by the software to update SRS15.DSS.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:30]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS26_HWINIT1</name>
              <description>HWINIT field</description>
              <bitRange>[29:26]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS26_SDCFSPV_25_16</name>
              <description>SDCFSPV## - SDCLK Clock Frequency Select - Preset Value

 This field can be used by the software to update SRS11.SDCFSH and SRS11.SDCFSL.</description>
              <bitRange>[25:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS26_DSSPV_15_14</name>
              <description>DSSPV## - Driver Strength Select - Preset Value

 This field can be used by the software to update SRS15.DSS.</description>
              <bitRange>[15:14]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS26_HWINIT0</name>
              <description>HWINIT field</description>
              <bitRange>[13:11]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS26_CGSPV_10</name>
              <description>CGSPV## - Clock Generator Select - Preset Value

 This field can be used by the software to update SRS11.CGS.</description>
              <bitRange>[10:10]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS26_SDCFSPV_09_00</name>
              <description>SDCFSPV## - SDCLK Clock Frequency Select - Preset Value

 This field can be used by the software to update SRS11.SDCFSH and SRS11.SDCFSL.</description>
              <bitRange>[9:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>SRS27</name>
          <description>SRS27 - Preset Value (SDR104 and DDR50)

 SRS27[15:0] - SDR104 if:
 SRS15.V18SE=1
 SRS15.UMS=011b

 SRS27[31:16] - DDR50 if:
 SRS15.V18SE=1
 SRS15.UMS=100b

 HWINIT Register - Note this register is hardware initialized after reset and the value read back will match the IP configuration.</description>
          <addressOffset>0x26C</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00020000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SRS27_DSSPV_31_30</name>
              <description>DSSPV## - Driver Strength Select - Preset Value

 This field can be used by the software to update SRS15.DSS.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:30]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS27_HWINIT1</name>
              <description>HWINIT field</description>
              <bitRange>[29:26]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS27_SDCFSPV_25_16</name>
              <description>SDCFSPV## - SDCLK Clock Frequency Select - Preset Value

 This field can be used by the software to update SRS11.SDCFSH and SRS11.SDCFSL.</description>
              <bitRange>[25:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS27_DSSPV_15_14</name>
              <description>DSSPV## - Driver Strength Select - Preset Value

 This field can be used by the software to update SRS15.DSS.</description>
              <bitRange>[15:14]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS27_HWINIT0</name>
              <description>HWINIT field</description>
              <bitRange>[13:10]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS27_SDCFSPV_09_00</name>
              <description>SDCFSPV## - SDCLK Clock Frequency Select - Preset Value

 This field can be used by the software to update SRS11.SDCFSH and SRS11.SDCFSL.</description>
              <bitRange>[9:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>SRS29</name>
          <description>SRS29 - Preset Value for UHS-II

 SRS29[15:0] - UHS-II if:
 SRS15.V18SE=0
 SRS15.UMS=111b

 HWINIT Register - Note this register is hardware initialized after reset and the value read back will match the IP configuration.</description>
          <addressOffset>0x274</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SRS29_HWINIT1</name>
              <description>HWINIT field</description>
              <bitRange>[31:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS29_DSSPV_15_14</name>
              <description>DSSPV## - Driver Strength Select - Preset Value

 This field can be used by the software to update SRS15.DSS.</description>
              <bitRange>[15:14]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS29_HWINIT0</name>
              <description>HWINIT field</description>
              <bitRange>[13:10]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRS29_SDCFSPV_09_00</name>
              <description>SDCFSPV## - SDCLK Clock Frequency Select - Preset Value

 This field can be used by the software to update SRS11.SDCFSH and SRS11.SDCFSL.</description>
              <bitRange>[9:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CQRS00</name>
          <description>CQRS00 - Command Queuing Version</description>
          <addressOffset>0x400</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000510</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CQVN1</name>
              <description>CQVN1 - eMMC Major Version Number

 Major version number of supported eMMC standard - 5.</description>
              <bitRange>[11:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CQVN2</name>
              <description>CQVN2 - eMMC Minor Version Number

 Minor version number of supported eMMC standard - 1.</description>
              <bitRange>[7:4]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CQVN3</name>
              <description>CQVN3 - eMMC Version Suffix

 Suffix version number of supported eMMC standard - 0.</description>
              <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CQRS01</name>
          <description>CQRS01 - Command Queuing Capabilities</description>
          <addressOffset>0x404</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ITCFMUL</name>
              <description>ITCFMUL - Internal Timer Clock Frequency Multiplier (ITCFMUL)

 Defines multiplier of internal clock frequency for the coalescing timer and for the SQS polling period.
 0 - 0.001 MHz
 1 - 0.01 MHz
 2 - 0.1 MHz
 3 - 1 MHz
 4 - 10 MHz

 The ITCFMUL and ITCFVAL defines the clock frequency.</description>
              <bitRange>[15:12]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ITCFVAL</name>
              <description>ITCFVAL - Internal Timer Clock Frequency Value (ITCFVAL)

 Value defines internal clock frequency for the coalescing timer and for the SQS polling period. The frequency is equal to ITCFMUL * ITCFVAL.</description>
              <bitRange>[9:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CQRS02</name>
          <description>CQRS02 - Command Queuing Configuration</description>
          <addressOffset>0x408</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CQDCE</name>
              <description>CQDCE - Direct Command (DCMD) Enable

 Process Task Descriptor for slot 31 as Data Transfer Task Descriptor (0) or Direct Command Task Descriptor (1).</description>
              <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CQTDS</name>
              <description>CQTDS - Task Descriptor

 Size Expect 128 bit (1) or 64 bit (0) task descriptor. This setting can be changed only when Command Queuing is disabled (CQE = 0).</description>
              <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CQE</name>
              <description>CQE - Command Queuing Enable

 Enables (1) or disables (0) the Command Queuing. This bit can be enabled only when all previous transactions are completed. This bit can be cleared only when all tasks are completed or cleared.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CQRS03</name>
          <description>CQRS03 - Command Queuing Control</description>
          <addressOffset>0x40C</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CQCAT</name>
              <description>CQCAT - Clear All Tasks

 Clears (1) all active tasks in the host controller. Software has to poll this register until operation is completed (bit is automatically cleared). Software can set this bit only when the CQ Engine is halted. Software has to clear all requested tasks in the eMMC device. Writing (0) has no effect.</description>
              <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CQHLT</name>
              <description>CQHLT - Halt CQ

 Engine can be halted by writing this bit 1. Any pending operation will be completed, and awaiting operation will be stopped. Once all tasks are completed or stopped this bit is set 1. The host controller will not automatically start any new operation, but software can use SRS registers to issue any command directly bypassing CQE. CQ Engine starts operation after being halted by writing 0 to this register. Writing 0 is ignored when CQ Engine is not halted.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CQRS04</name>
          <description>CQRS04 - Command Queuing Interrupt Status
 This register has several status bit related to specific interrupt event. When even happened and related Command Queuing Interrupt Status Enable is set, the status bit is set to 1. The bits can be cleared by S/W.
 Write 0 clears bit.
 Write 1 is ignored.</description>
          <addressOffset>0x410</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CQTCL</name>
              <description>CQTCL - Task Cleared (TCL)

 When task clear operation or clear individual task is completed, the CQE sets this bit to 1.</description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CQREDI</name>
              <description>CQREDI - Response Error Detected Interrupt (RED)

 When an error is detected in the response received from eMMC device, the CQE sets this bit to 1. S/W can select which bits are analyzed by selecting CQRMEM.</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CQTCC</name>
              <description>CQTCC - Task Complete Interrupt (TCC)

 CQE sets this bit when either a task with INT=1 is completed or Interrupt Coalescing reports interrupt.</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CQHAC</name>
              <description>CQHAC - Halt Complete Interrupt (HAC)

 CQE sets this bit when value of CQHLT changed from 0 to 1.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CQRS05</name>
          <description>CQRS05 - Command Queuing Interrupt Status Enable

 Statuses Enable bits enables interrupt sources. The status is enabled when bit is set 1 (S/W wrote 1 to the field).</description>
          <addressOffset>0x414</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CQTCLST</name>
              <description>CQTCLST - Task Cleared Status Enable (TCL)

 Enables CQTCL register.</description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CQREDST</name>
              <description>CQREDST - Response Error Detected Status Enable (RED)

 Enables CQREDI register.</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CQTCCST</name>
              <description>CQTCCST - Task Complete Status Enable (TCC)

 Enables CQHAC register.</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CQHACST</name>
              <description>CQHACST - Halt Complete Status Enable (HAC)

 Enables CQTCLST register.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CQRS06</name>
          <description>CQRS06 - Command Queuing Interrupt Signal Enable
 This register allows to turn on or turn off interrupt notification separately for each bit of the Command Queuing Interrupt Status. When Interrupt status bit is set 1 and related field in this register is set (S/W wrote 1 to the filed), the Interrupt Status is reported on interrupt port.</description>
          <addressOffset>0x418</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CQTCLSI</name>
              <description>CQTCLSI - Task Cleared Signal Enable (TCL)

 Enables interrupt signaling from CQTCL register.</description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CQREDSI</name>
              <description>CQREDSI - Response Error Detected Signal Enable (TCC)

 Enables interrupt signaling from CQREDI register.</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CQTCCSI</name>
              <description>CQTCCSI - Task Complete Signal Enable (TCC)

 Enables interrupt signaling from CQTCC register.</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CQHACSI</name>
              <description>CQHACSI - Halt Complete Signal Enable (HAC)

 Enables interrupt signaling from CQHLT register.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CQRS07</name>
          <description>CQRS07 - Interrupt Coalescing

 This register allows to group a CQ transfer and report single interrupt for entire group of requested tasks.</description>
          <addressOffset>0x41C</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CQICED</name>
              <description>CQICED - Interrupt Coalescing Enable/Disable

 Enables coalescing mechanism allowing to generate coalescing interrupts.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CQICSB</name>
              <description>CQICSB - Interrupt Coalescing Status Bit (ICSB)

 CQE sets this bit 1 when any task with INT=0 is completed.</description>
              <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CQICCTR</name>
              <description>CQICCTR - Counter and Timer Reset(ICCTR)

 S/W resets interrupt coalescing timer and counter.</description>
              <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CQICCTHWEN</name>
              <description>CQICCTHWEN - Interrupt Coalescing Counter Threshold Write Enable (ICCTHWEN)

 This is write enable for CQICCTH. When this bit is set 1, the field will be updated.</description>
              <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CQICCTH</name>
              <description>CQICCTH - Interrupt Coalescing Counter Threshold (ICCTH)

 CQE increments internal counter when task with INT=0 is completed. When internal counter reaches this value the coalescing generates interrupt. S/W can select treshold value in range 1 to 31. S/W can disable internal counter and interrupt generation by setting this field to 0.</description>
              <bitRange>[12:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CQICTOVALEN</name>
              <description>CQICTOVALEN - Interrupt Coalescing Timeout Value Write Enable (ICTOVALWEN)

 This is write enable for CQICTOVAL. When this bit is set 1, the field will be updated.</description>
              <bitRange>[7:7]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CQICTOVAL</name>
              <description>CQICTOVAL - Interrupt Coalescing Timeout Value (ICTOVAL)

 CQE generates interrupt when internal counter reaches period defined in this field. The counter starts when first transfer with INT=0 is completed. The counter increments each time when Internal Clock * 1024 period elapsed. S/W can disable this timer by setting this filed to 0.</description>
              <bitRange>[6:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CQRS08</name>
          <description>CQRS08 - Command Queuing Task Descriptor List Base Address</description>
          <addressOffset>0x420</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CQTDLBA</name>
              <description>CQTDLBA - Task Descriptor List Base Address (lower)

 Base address (32 lower bits) of the Task Descriptor List. S/W will write values aligned to 1kB boundary (lower 10 bits have to be 0). The hardware ignores 10 lower bits.
 S/W will update this register only when CQE is disabled.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CQRS09</name>
          <description>CQRS09 - Command Queuing Task Descriptor List Base Address Upper 32 Bits</description>
          <addressOffset>0x424</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CQTDLBAU</name>
              <description>CQTDLBAU - Task Descriptor List Base Address (upper)

 Base address (32 upper bits) of the Task descriptor List. This register is not used in 32 bit addressing mode (S/W does not change this value). S/W will update this register only when CQE is disabled.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CQRS10</name>
          <description>CQRS10 - Command Queuing Task Doorbell
 CQ has 32 tasks have individual bits to start operation on desired task. The S/W writes 1 on any position from 0 to 31 to start task 0 to 31. The S/W can request more than one task in single write. The CQ Engine process tasks in order they were requested:
 - when more than one task is requested in single register write, the task with lower number has higher priority over task with higher number
 - task(s) requested in earlier register write has higher priority over task(s) in later register write
 The order of the tasks are maintained during all phases of transaction. If given task is not ready for execution, the CQ Engine takes next task with highest number.
 CQ Engine needs several clock cycles to push requested in the single register write Task Doorbell to queue. The slave interface ends write transfer as soon all tasks are in the queue.
 When S/W writes 0 to bit in this register, the related task won't start - this value is ignored.
 Task Doorbell bit remain 1 until task execution is completed, task is cleared by Clear All Task or Clear Task with this number or CQ Engine is disabled (CQE=0).</description>
          <addressOffset>0x428</addressOffset>
